.TH MM41 1 "November 2026" "MM-41 2.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mm41 \- programmable RPN calculator for real, complex and decimal scalars, strings and matrices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mm41
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR EXPR ]...
.RB [ \-f
.IR FILE ]...
.RI [ SCRIPT
.RI [ ARG ]...]
.br
.B mm41
.BR \-\-modules " | " \-\-commands " | " \-\-commands-tex " | " \-\-version " | " \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mm41
is a modular stack-based reverse Polish notation calculator in the spirit
of the HP-41C and FORTH; it unifies the earlier MM-11, MM-14, MM-15 and
MM-B1 calculators. The stack holds real, complex and exact decimal
scalars, strings, and real and complex matrices (GNU Scientific Library).
It has 64 storage registers, user-defined words with conditionals and
loops, numerical integration, root finding, minimisation and
differentiation over those words, polynomial roots, dates and
sunrise/sunset, workdays with holiday calendars, a finance module (time
value of money, amortisation,
NPV/IRR, bonds, depreciation, day counts) computing in 34-digit decimals,
and HP-41 style programs loaded from files.
.PP
With no arguments and a terminal on standard input it runs interactively
with GNU readline editing, history and tab completion; the stack is
printed after every line. Otherwise it runs in batch mode: the given
expressions and files, or standard input, are evaluated, the final stack is
printed, and the exit status is 1 if any error occurred.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI SCRIPT " [ARG]..."
run the lines of SCRIPT (a first line #!/usr/bin/env mm41 makes it a
program); the arguments are pushed first, numbers as numbers, the rest as
strings; quiet unless -p: a script prints what it wants with
.BR . .
.TP
.B \-
read the script from standard input.
.TP
.B \-q
quiet: do not print the final stack.
.TP
.B \-p
print the top of the stack plainly at the end (a number as a number, a
matrix as rows of numbers); implies -q.
.TP
.B \-x
stop at the first error.
.TP
.BI \-e " EXPR"
Evaluate
.I EXPR
as one line of input. May be repeated; expressions run in order.
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Run the lines of
.IR FILE ;
.B \-
means standard input. May be repeated and mixed with
.BR \-e .
.TP
.B \-n
Do not load the saved words, the modules in the config directory, or the config at startup.
.TP
.B \-\-modules
List the built-in modules and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-commands
Print the complete command reference as Markdown (this is how COMMANDS.md
is generated) and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-commands\-tex
Print the command reference as LaTeX tables (used to build the manual) and
exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH INPUT
Input is whitespace-separated tokens. All input is case sensitive.
.TP
.B Numbers
.BR 3 ", " \-1.2e\-4 ", " .5 ;
with the suffix
.B d
.RB ( 0.1d )
a 34-digit decimal, with
.B b
a binary double. In decimal mode
.RB ( decimal " / " binary )
every literal is a decimal.
.TP
.B Complex numbers
.BR (1,2) ", " "(\-1.2e\-4, 0.7e2)"
(spaces inside the parentheses are allowed)
.TP
.B Strings
.BR \(dqhello\(dq ,
with the escapes \e\(dq \e\e \en \et.
.TP
.B Matrices
.BR "[1 2 3]" " (a row vector), " "[1 2; 3 4]" " (a semicolon ends a row), or " "[rows cols $ elements...]"
with real or complex elements;
.BR "\(dqfile\(dq readmat" ", " readvec " or " "\(dqfile\(dq j readcol"
to read a table, every number, or one column from a file; or
.B [rows, cols, \(dqfile\(dq]
to read one from a whitespace-separated text file.
.TP
.B Comments
.B \e
or
.B #
to the end of the line.
.TP
.B Execution tokens
.B @name
pushes a token for a command or word; the numerical methods and
.B exec
consume it.
.SH ERRORS AND UNDO
Each top-level token is atomic. If it fails, the stack is restored to its
state before that token, the error is printed on standard error, and the
rest of the line is skipped. A user word is one token for this purpose: a
word that fails half-way leaves no trace. In the REPL,
.B undo
restores the stack as it was before the previous line.
.PP
Real functions whose result would not be real promote their argument to
complex, consistently:
.B \-1 sqrt
is
.BR (0,1) ,
.B 2 asin
is
.BR (1.5708,1.3170) ,
.B \-8 3 inv ^
is
.BR (1,1.7321) ,
and a real matrix with one such entry becomes a complex matrix. Division by
zero is an error.
.SH COMMANDS
The full list, with stack pictures, descriptions and examples, is printed
by
.B listfcns
(grouped by category) and by
.BR "mm41 \-\-commands" ;
.B help name
(or
.BR "\(dqname\(dq usage" )
describes one command in full: each stack entry it takes, what it leaves,
and its example, run when harmless;
.B help module
and
.B help category
list a module or a category,
.B help word
shows a user word, and
.B help text
finds the commands whose name or description mentions the text.
.B manual
opens the user manual (mm41-manual.txt, installed with the PDF) in the
pager,
.B manual survival
at that chapter and
.B manual npv
at the first mention; n finds the next, q returns.
A line that is only
.BR use ", " unuse ", " load ", " run ", " batch " or " usehol
and one bare name is read as the string form:
.B use const
is
.BR "\(dqconst\(dq use" ,
.B usehol us
is
.BR "\(dqus\(dq usehol" .
.B skin hp41
(or
.BR hp12c ", " hp48 ", " hp48px ", " plain )
draws the stack listing as that calculator's display would have: a
twelve-character LCD with annunciators, a seven-segment display, the 48's
screen (hp48px: as a dot matrix in block pixels). Only the listing changes; the setting
is saved in config.txt.
TAB after one of them completes the name, and after
.B help
also module, topic and category names. The
categories are
Arithmetic, Exp/Log, Trigonometry, Decimal, Finance, Complex, Special
functions, Constants, Logic, Stack, Strings, Matrices, Polynomials,
Numerics, Registers, Dates, Display, Words, Programs and Session.
.SS Stack
.B dup drop swap over nip tuck pick roll depth clst print ps pm savestack loadstack.
.B n pick
copies the item at depth n (0 = top);
.B n roll
moves it to the top (1 roll = swap, 2 roll = rot).
.SS Registers
.B value n sto
stores
.I value
in register
.I n
(0\-63), as on the HP;
.B n rcl
recalls a copy. Registers hold any type.
.B pr
shows which are in use;
.B saveregs
and
.B loadregs
persist them.
.SS Decimals
A decimal combined with a real gives a decimal (the real is converted by
its shortest round-trip form), so
.B 0.1d 0.2 +
is exactly 0.3. Arithmetic, percentages, comparisons, registers and the
control words accept decimals; trigonometry, complex numbers, matrices and
the numerical methods are binary and reject a decimal value with a message
naming
.BR >bin ,
while a decimal given as an index, count, dimension, precision or flag is
converted.
.B >dec
and
.B >bin
convert;
.B x n round
rounds half-even to n places;
.B isdec
and
.B decmode
test. Fixed-point display rounds to the
.B setprec
places; scientific display shows a decimal as stored.
.SS Finance
Every finance word computes in decimals and leaves a decimal; rates are in
percent per period; cash received is positive. Time value of money:
.B n i% pv pmt fv g%
store the registers
.RB ( g%
is the growth of the payment per period: payment t is pmt(1+g)^(t-1), 0 for a level annuity),
.B ?n ?i% ?pv ?pmt ?fv ?g%
solve for one of them,
.BR beg / end
set the payment timing,
.B clrtvm
and
.B tvm
clear and show them;
.B amort
prints the amortisation schedule and
.B amortm
pushes it as an n x 4 matrix.
.B cf rate npv
and
.B cf irr
take a cash-flow vector in time order (or an N x 2 matrix of amount and
count).
.BR "nom m nom>eff" ", " "eff m eff>nom" ;
.BR "cost salvage life sl" ", " "... year soyd" ", " "... year factor db" ;
.BR "cpn yld n redeem bprice" ", " "price cpn n redeem byield" .
Dated words take dates as \(dqd.m.yyyy\(dq strings or YYYYMMDD numbers
.RB ( date>ymd ", " ymd>date
convert):
.B M rate xnpv
and
.B M xirr
over an N x 2 matrix of amount and date (actual/365 from the first date);
.BR "settle maturity cpn yld redeem freq basis xprice" ,
.BR "... cpn price redeem freq basis xyield" ,
.BR "settle maturity cpn freq basis xaccr" ,
.B d1 d2 basis yearfrac
with basis 0 = 30/360, 1 = actual/actual, 2 = actual/360, 3 = actual/365,
4 = 30E/360.
.nf
clrtvm 12 n 1 i% 10000 pv 0 fv ?pmt      \-888.49
[6 1 $ \-1000 300 300 300 300 300] 10 npv  137.24
20080215 20171115 5.75 6.5 100 2 0 xprice 94.6344
.fi
.SS Gross and net pay
.B """si"" usetax"
(Slovenia) or
.B """us"" usetax"
(United States, federal) selects the tax schedule of the newest year built
in, and the choice is kept in config.txt;
.B """us-2025"" usetax"
selects a given year. Amounts are per month for Slovenia and per year for the
United States
.RB ( permonth ,
.BR peryear ).
.B "gross net"
is the net pay,
.B "net gross"
the gross pay that leaves it;
.BR tax ,
.BR contrib ,
.B cost
(to the employer),
.BR wedge ,
.B atr
and
.B mtr
(average and marginal rates in percent) take a gross pay too, each
elementwise on a real matrix;
.B payslip
prints the calculation and
.B taxinfo
the schedule.
.BR """mfj"" filing" ,
.B "n children"
and
.B "n dependants"
set the taxpayer's circumstances.
.B """si-2026"" taxfile"
writes the built-in schedule to
.I tax/si-2026.txt
in the configuration directory, where an edited copy is read in preference
to the built-in one.
.B "x M btax"
is the tax on x by any scale (rows of from, rate in percent).
.SS Words
.nf
: name body ;        \e" one line; redefinition replaces
\(dqname\(dq usage        \e" show the definition
listwords  n delword  clrwords  savewords  loadwords
.fi
A word is compiled when defined: unknown names are rejected at once, and
control flow becomes branches. A word may call itself; nesting stops at
256. Control words (inside definitions only):
.nf
flag if ... [else ...] then
begin ... flag until
begin ... flag while ... repeat
limit start do ... i ... loop        \e" skipped when start = limit
limit start do ... n +loop           \e" n < 0 counts down to limit inclusive
.fi
.B i
is the innermost loop index,
.B j
the next outer one. Flags are real numbers; 0 is false.
.PP
.nf
: fac  dup 1 gt if dup 1 \- fac * then ;
: sum  0 swap 1 + 1 do i + loop ;
.fi
.SS Modules
Every command belongs to a module;
.B modules
lists them with versions, and
.B \(dqname\(dq usage
names the module of a command. Built-in modules are compiled in
(\fBmake MODULES=...\fR chooses which). A
.I word module
is a file of definitions, one per line as \fIname body\fR or
\fB: \fIname body\fB ;\fR, with an optional first line
.BR "\e module NAME VERSION SUMMARY" ;
every
.I modules/*.mm
in the configuration directory is loaded at startup, and
.B \(dqname\(dq use
/
.B \(dqname\(dq unuse
load and unload one by name or path. The shipped
.B std
module (NPV, IRR, cumprod, sto_add, norms, locations, ...) is one;
.B listmacros
lists the words of the loaded word modules. A
.I compiled module
is a shared object exporting
.BR mm41_module() ;
.B \(dqfile.so\(dq load
loads one, as does placing it in
.IR modules/ ;
.B make shared-module DIR=...
builds one (see
.IR examples/modules/hello ).
.SS The tutorial
.B tutor
opens a book of fifteen short chapters you type along with: each step shows a
passage and a line to type, and the tutor moves on when the stack shows what the
lesson expects (skip, back, again, retry; q leaves; progress is kept in config.txt).
.B tutor
.I n
or
.B tutor finance
opens a chapter,
.B tutor list
shows them.
.B examples
.I n
prints a chapter as a cookbook, every line with its result;
.B mm41 --tutor-check
verifies the whole book against the program.
.SS Numerical methods
A function is any word or command mapping x on the stack to f(x), usually a
word of your own defined on a line of its own:
.B ": f dup * 2 - ;"
then
.BR "0 3 @f fzero" .
A parameter is fixed inside the word
.RB ( ": j2 2 besselj ;"
then
.BR "0 10 @j2 integrate" )
or kept in a register
.RB ( ": jn 0 rcl besselj ;" ).
.B help
.I command
shows such an example for every command that takes @f.
.nf
a b @f integrate     integral over [a,b] (tanh-sinh, bisecting where needed)
a b @f romberg       the same by Romberg extrapolation
a b @f fzero         root in a bracket with a sign change (Brent)
g   @f solve         root near one guess (bracket search, then Brent)
a b @f minimize      argument of the minimum in [a,b] (Brent)
x   @f deriv         f'(x) (Ridders' extrapolation)
lo hi @f fsum        sum of f(i), i = lo..hi;  fprod: product
@f exec              run f
.fi
.B x tol
sets the tolerance (default 1e-10, relative to max(1,|result|));
.B nevals
and
.B interr
report the evaluations used and the error estimate of the last integral.
The function must leave exactly one real; an error inside it aborts the
call. An integrable endpoint singularity converges to full precision when
that endpoint is 0.
.SS Statistics, random draws, differential equations
Vectors are N x 1 or 1 x N real matrices.
.B v mean sd sem median skew kurt ,
.B v p quantile ,
.B x y cov corr ,
.B v n movavg ,
.B v k hist ;
fits
.B x y linfit expfit logfit powfit
leave [a b r2];
.B X y ols
(intercept added) and
.B X y olstab
(coefficient, standard error, t, p; R^2). Distributions
.B tcdf tinv chi2cdf chi2inv fcdf finv binompdf binomcdf poispdf poiscdf .
Random:
.B n seed ,
one draw
.B rnorm runif rpois rexp ,
n draws
.B normrnd unifrnd poisrnd binomrnd exprnd trnd chi2rnd ,
.B resample shuffle ,
.B n @f sims
(run a word n times, collect its result),
.B v mcstats .
Differential equations:
.B y0 t0 t1 n @f odeint
and
.B y0 t0 t1 @f odesol
with the word
.BR "t y -- y'" ,
scalar or system (y a row vector).
.SS Survival analysis
Select a lifetime law once,
.BR "\(dqmakeham\(dq [a b c] setlaw"
(exponential, weibull, gompertz, makeham, siler, loglogistic, lognormal,
gamma), then
.BR "t surv haz cumhaz lifepdf lifecdf" ,
.BR "p lifeinv" ,
.BR "lifemean lifemed lifevar lifemode" ,
.B t elife
(expected remaining life),
.BR "x n tpx nqx" ,
.BR "x px qx lx" ,
.B x0 x1 lifetab
(age, lx, dx, qx, ex),
.B x i ax
and
.B x i Ax
(annuity-due and whole-life insurance of 1, term versions
.BR axn ", " Axn ).
From data (times, event 1 / censored 0):
.BR "t e km" ", " "t e nelsonaalen" ", " "t1 e1 t2 e2 logrank" ", " "t e lifefit"
(maximum likelihood); from a life table
.BR "x q lawfit" ", " "x l lawfitl" .
.SS Income distribution
Select a family once,
.BR "\(dqdagum\(dq [a b p] setdist"
(pareto, lognormal, dagum, singhmaddala, fisk, gamma, weibull), then
.BR "x incpdf inccdf" ", " "p incq" ", " "incmean incvar incmed incmode" ,
.BR "gini theil mld" ", " "eps atkinson" ", " "p lorenz" ", " "p top" ", " palma ", " "p1 p2 qratio" ,
.BR "z headcount povgap" ", " "z alpha fgt" ", " "v incfit" .
The same from a vector of incomes:
.BR "ginid theild mldd atkinsond lorenzd decilesd palmad topd headcountd povgapd fgtd" ;
from published quintile or decile shares
.BR "v incfits" ,
from Lorenz points
.BR "p L incfitl" .
.SS Graphs
.BR "x y plot" ", " scatter ", " steps ", " "x h bars" ", " "v k hplot" ", " "M mplot" ", " "a b xt fplot"
draw in the terminal;
.B hold
overlays,
.BR "\(dqs\(dq ptitle pxlabel pylabel plegend" ", " "pxlog pylog plin" ", " pgrid ", " "a b pxrange pyrange" ", " "w h psize"
decorate, and
.B \(dqfig.svg\(dq psvg
writes the figure as vector graphics.
.SS Workdays
Dates are \(dqd.m.yyyy\(dq strings or YYYYMMDD numbers.
.B d1 d2 workdays
counts the workdays from d1 to d2, both inclusive (Excel NETWORKDAYS;
negative when d2 precedes d1);
.B date n workdayplus
is the date n workdays later (WORKDAY; the start day is not counted);
.B date isworkday
tests one date. A workday is a Monday to Friday not in the holiday
calendar, which is empty until
.B \(dqus\(dq usehol
(the federal holidays, observed), 
.B \(dqsi\(dq usehol
(Slovenia) or
.B \(dqname\(dq usehol
from
.I ~/.config/mm41/holidays/name.txt
(one date per line, # comments) installs one and records it in
.IR config.txt .
.B \(dqus\(dq holidays
and
.B year \(dqsi\(dq yholidays
push a calendar as a vector of YYYYMMDD,
.B H sethol
installs any such vector for the session,
.B hol
shows the installed calendar and
.B clrhol
removes it.
.SS Programs
.B \(dqfile\(dq run
loads and runs an HP-41 style program: one instruction per line, with
.BR "LBL name" ", " "GOTO name" ", " "GOSUB name" ", " RTN ", " END
and tests ending in
.B ?
that skip the next line when false.
.B \(dqfile\(dq batch
runs a file of ordinary lines;
.B \(dqtext\(dq eval
evaluates a string.
.SS Shell
A line starting with
.B !
runs the rest as a shell command and pushes its output as a string.
.SH FILES
All in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mm41/ ,
which defaults to
.IR ~/.config/mm41/ :
.TP
.I config.txt
print precision, fixed/scientific, number mode, holiday calendar, tax schedule,
tolerance; written at exit.
.TP
.I tax/*.txt
tax schedules of your own, or edited copies of the built-in ones (see
.BR taxfile ).
.TP
.I holidays/*.txt
holiday calendars; the shipped
.I example.txt
is replaced by
.BR "make install" ,
your own files are kept.
.TP
.I modules/*.mm, modules/*.so
word modules and compiled modules, loaded at startup in name order.
.B make install
replaces the shipped word modules (such as
.IR std.mm ),
so keep your own words in
.I user_words.txt
or in a module of your own.
.TP
.I user_words.txt
words saved by
.BR savewords ,
loaded at startup.
.TP
.IR registers.txt ", " stack_backup.dat ", " .rpn_history
registers, saved stack and readline history.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; in batch mode 1 if any error was reported, 2 for a usage
error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ mm41 \-e \(dq2 3 + 4 *\(dq
[0] ℝ : 20.000000
$ mm41 \-n \-e \(dq[2 2 $ 4 7 2 6] minv pm\(dq
$ mm41 \-n \-e \(dq: f dup * 2 \- ;\(dq \-e \(dq0 3 @f fzero\(dq
[0] ℝ : 1.414214
$ echo \(dq(1,2) (3,4) *\(dq | mm41 \-n
$ mm41 \-n \-e \(dq2 setprec clrtvm 360 n 0.5 i% 300000 pv 0 fv ?pmt\(dq
[0] 𝔻 : \-1798.65
.fi
